Title: Autotune the test suite's parallelism to the host's cores
Created: 2026-10-25
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-10-25T06:26Z claude created
2026-10-26T17:32Z reviewer coder: measurements, rule and audit extension recorded; doudou sweep incomplete (host unreachable)

--- body ---
## Context

The full test gate hard-codes its parallelism, and the hosts differ: doudou has 4 cores, padme has 32.

Read in the repo: `pyproject.toml` pins `pytest-xdist` with a comment that `-n 4` measured 81 s to 38.6 s (ticket 1011);
the `Makefile` target `check-tests` uses `-n 8` (46.9 s at `-n 4` to 33.8 s at `-n 8`, three trials, load 7 to 9 on padme);
CI keeps its own `-n 4` and says the forge runner's cores were not measured; `check-fast` and `lint` stay serial. The fall-back
to serial when xdist is missing is already in the Makefile.

`-n 8` on a 4-core host oversubscribes; `-n 8` on a 32-core host leaves most cores idle (derived from the figures above, not
measured at other values). `-n auto` counts logical CPUs (64 on padme), which is also unmeasured here.

Related, from the arena (ticket 1094): on padme, test time was 30 to 66 % of an agent leg's wall-clock, and the 3975WX
(32 cores) is not faster than the 3945WX it replaced on a single core, so test time is where parallelism matters most.
The agent legs run in other repositories, so this ticket concerns the IDH suite and the pattern, not the arena's targets.

## Design

Compute `-n` from the number of physical cores with a floor and a cap and an environment override, in one place that the
Makefile (and CI, if it can read it) share. The rule is chosen from measurements on both hosts, not from this text.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] (padme done; doudou: one real trial, rest by a padme stand-in, see Measurements) Measured wall-clock of the full gate at several `-n` values on padme (32 cores) and doudou (4 cores), three trials each, recorded in the ticket
- [x] A rule that picks `-n` from the host, with the override and the serial fall-back, and a test that pins the rule
- [x] The worker-safety audit of ticket 1011 extended to the highest `-n` the rule can pick
- [x] Makefile and pyproject comments point to the one rule instead of stating numbers

## Measurements (2026-10-26, ticket 1215 coder)

Full suite (`pytest tests/ -n N`, 2123 to 2130 tests, 4 skipped on padme), tree at f521bd82 plus the new test file.
Wall-clock in seconds, mean of three trials unless noted. Load average was 4 to 48 throughout: padme was shared with
other sessions, so differences under about 5 s are inside the noise.

padme (Threadripper PRO 3975WX, 32 cores, 64 threads):

| -n | mean (min-max) |
|---|---|
| serial | 156.3 (150.0-164.4) |
| 2 | 84.6 (83.5-86.5) |
| 4 | 48.1 (46.7-50.1) |
| 8 | 30.9 (30.7-31.1) |
| 12 | 27.1 (24.2-30.1) |
| 16 | 32.7 (31.4-34.8) |
| 24 | 23.4 (23.2-23.5) |
| 32 | 29.2 (27.3-32.2) |
| 64 | 33.1 (30.3-35.1) |

Because the sweep is non-monotonic, a second sweep interleaved 8, 16, 24 over five rounds (mean of five): 8 = 34.9 s,
16 = 34.3 s, 24 = 25.5 s. The 24 advantage (about 9 s, 27 %) reproduced; 32 and 64 were slower than 24 in the first sweep.
The cause of the dip at 24 is not established (scheduling of the three shell monoliths is a candidate).

4-core stand-in (derived, not doudou): padme pinned with `taskset -c 0-3,32-35` (4 cores, 8 threads, but Zen 2 cores,
faster than doudou's i5-8250U): -n 2 = 84.7, -n 4 = 51.4, -n 8 = 45.3, -n 12 = 49.3.
SMT threads paid (-n 8 beats -n 4 by 12 %), oversubscription past the thread count did not.

doudou (i5-8250U, 4 cores, 8 threads), real: one trial at -n 4 = 94.5 s (load 4.1; 2037 passed, 90 skipped, the skips
are host-dependent). The remaining trials were started, then the host dropped off the network (no route on port 22)
and was not reachable again in this session; the doudou sweep is the one open item of the first exit criterion.

## Rule

`scripts/pytest_workers.py`: usable CPUs (affinity-aware logical CPUs), floor 2, cap 24; `IDH_TEST_WORKERS=N` overrides
(0 = serial); no output (serial) when pytest-xdist is not importable. On padme it picks 24 (best measured), on a
4-core/8-thread host 8 (best measured in the stand-in), on a 2-core/4-thread CI runner 4 (what CI used; unmeasured).
Rejected: `-n auto` (64 on padme, 30 % slower than 24), physical cores (32 on padme, 4 on a 4-core: both worse than
the logical-CPU pick in the data above).

## Worker-safety audit extension (to -n 24)

Static census re-run over tests/ at f521bd82 for the three 1011 axes: sockets bind port 0 (tests/test_net_relay.py,
tests/zotero_live.py), fixed `/tmp` paths are read-only or inert strings (test_on_end_hook.py, test_hook_launchers.py),
no `git config --global`, no ambient `os.environ` writes outside monkeypatch. Live: 33 unpinned runs at -n 8 to 64 on padme (8 at -n 24, 6 at
-n 32 or 64) returned the same pass/skip counts (2130 passed, 4 skipped); every run from -n 12 up passed. The red runs (serial 2 and 3, -n 2 and 4, one -n 8: nine runs) all ran while this change's own files were mid-edit: a marker violation
in the new test and a Makefile-shape test not yet updated; both are the same two assertions, fixed before the gate.
